  <content>
  <section><emphasis><emphasis>
    <para>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</para>
  </emphasis></emphasis>
<para>1. What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph that represents \(y=(x−2)^2−8\)?</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 2. The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex is found by using the squared term of the vertex form. The value of the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is 2</para>
<para>2. What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph that represents \(y=(x−2)^2−8\)?</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -8. In vertex form, the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex is the constant. The value of the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph is -8.</para>
<para>3. Where is the \(y\)-intercept for the equation \(y=(x−2)^2−8\) located?</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -4. Substitute 0 for \(x\) and you get \(y=(0−2)^2−8=4−8=-4\).</para>
<para>4. Which of the following is another point on the graph of the equation \(y=(x−2)^2−8\)?</para>
<list>
  <item> \((4,-4)\) </item>
  <item> \((-4,4)\) </item>
  <item> \((4,4)\) </item>
  <item> \((-4,-4)\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((4,-4)\)</para>
<para>5. Which graph is a representation of \(y=(x - 6)^2 - 5\)?</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e98f1e12af7c125e63a0ff37b612b77c75365e21"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Graph A</para>
<para>6. The function \(v\) is defined by \(v(x)=12(x+5)^2−7\).</para>
<para>Without graphing, determine if the vertex of the graph representing \(v\) shows the minimum or maximum value of the function.</para>
<list>
  <item> The vertex shows the minimum value. </item>
  <item> The vertex shows the maximum value. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The vertex shows the minimum value.</para>
<para>7. Choose the equation that matches the graph of parabola D.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e98f1e12af7c125e63a0ff37b612b77c75365e21"/></media></figure></para>
<list>
  <item> \(y=-2(x−6)^2−5\) </item>
  <item> \(y=(x−6)^2−5\) </item>
  <item> \(y=6(x−6)^2−5\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-\frac {1}{3}(x−6)^2−5\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=-\frac {1}{3}(x−6)^2−5\)</para>
<para>8. Choose the equation that matches the graph B.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e98f1e12af7c125e63a0ff37b612b77c75365e21"/></media></figure></para>
<list>
  <item> \(y=-2(x−6)^2−5\) </item>
  <item> \(y=(x−6)^2−5\) </item>
  <item> \(y=6(x−6)^2−5\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-\frac {1}{3}(x−6)^2−5\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=-2(x−6)^2−5\)</para>
<para>9. Choose the equation that matches the graph C.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/e98f1e12af7c125e63a0ff37b612b77c75365e21"/></media></figure></para>
<list>
  <item> \(y=-2(x−6)^2−5\) </item>
  <item> \(y=(x−6)^2−5\) </item>
  <item> \(y=6(x−6)^2−5\) </item>
  <item> \(y=-\frac {1}{3}(x−6)^2−5\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(y=6(x−6)^2−5\)</para></section>
